# --- §4 auditor read allowlist ---
RED_FIRST=tests/test_legacy_residue_gate.py::test_migration_read_allowlist_is_a_closed_set_of_named_readers
  FAILED (shipped set was only {_read_secure_json,_read_secure_bytes})
ADDED_3_OF_4=
  _capture_owned_artifact    — os.lstat → reject symlink/non-regular → require mode 0600 and
    st_uid==geteuid() → path.read_bytes() → sha256, optionally compared to an expected digest;
    returns _OwnedArtifactIdentity (name/dev/ino/sha/mode/owner), never file content. SAFE WRAPPER.
  _matches_artifact_identity — os.lstat → reject symlink/non-regular → require dev/ino/mode/owner
    to equal a previously captured identity → path.read_bytes() → returns only bool
    (sha256 == expected). SAFE WRAPPER.
  _file_sha256_if_regular    — _exists → os.lstat → reject symlink/non-regular →
    path.read_bytes() → returns Optional[str] digest only. SAFE WRAPPER.
NOT_ADDED_1_OF_4=
  _compensate_to_prestate    — NOT a read wrapper. It is a ~160-line recovery orchestrator that
    inlines a bare `bak.read_bytes()` (migration.py:2038) and feeds the real bytes to
    _write_bytes_temp to republish the formal file. The read is digest-guarded by
    _matches_owned(bak, sha) but bypasses the designated private reader, which contradicts the
    allowlist's own stated invariant ("Only private byte/JSON reader entry points may open/read
    old dual files"). Per task §4 last paragraph it was NOT whitelisted and is reported instead.
BOUNDARY_KEPT=only literal symbol names added to ALLOWED_DUAL_FILE_READ_SYMBOLS; no rule change,
  no prefix/regex/whole-module matching, no ALLOWED_DUAL_FILE_SYMBOLS change
MUT4=tests/test_legacy_residue_gate.py::test_mutation_wildcard_migration_read_allowlist_is_red —
