--- Step 5 (§4.5 F-3): _compensate_to_prestate bare read ---------------------
Location: credential_guard/migration.py L2026 (task said L2038; line numbers had shifted).
Change: bak.read_bytes()  ->  _read_secure_bytes(bak).raw     (exactly one call site)

EXCEPTION-TYPE ANALYSIS (task §4.5 required this before changing anything):
  The task's premise was inaccurate on two points, verified by reading both call sites:
  (a) The old bare read raised OSError, which is NOT caught by the enclosing
      `except MigrationError:` at L2040. It escaped _compensate_to_prestate entirely,
      and escaped BOTH call sites too, because both guard only MigrationError:
        migrate_config L1163-1166  -> `except MigrationError: recovery_failed = True`
        _recover_journal_or_raise L2152-2155 -> `except MigrationError: recovery_err = True`
      A raw OSError therefore propagated out of migrate_config and was only swallowed far
      away by run_migrate_config's `except Exception: run_err = "failed"`, printing the
      generic "migrate-config failed" with no error code.
  (b) MIGRATION_RESTORE is never the externally visible code from this path. It is only a
      fail_code handed to _publish_no_clobber / _unlink_temp_after_publish, and L2048
      converts every MigrationError in this block into MIGRATION_RECOVERY_REQUIRED.
  After the change: _read_secure_bytes raises MigrationError -> caught at L2040 ->
  restore_err=True -> MIGRATION_RECOVERY_REQUIRED, i.e. the SAME externally visible code as
  every other failure in this function, and the same code the publish-failure branch already
  produced. So the error branch moves from "uncaught OSError / generic failed" to the
  structured fail-closed code. This is a strict improvement, not a regression, and the outer
  excepts now handle it correctly where before they did not.
  Added checks: _read_secure_bytes enforces mode 0o600, euid ownership and MAX_V1_FILE_BYTES
  on top of the pre-existing _matches_owned check (which only rejects symlink/non-regular).
  Legitimate baks are renamed from v1 sources already validated at 0600, so the happy path is
  unchanged; a tampered bak now fails closed instead of being restored.

RECOVERY-PATH TDD EVIDENCE (before -> after the change, identical selection):
  pytest tests/test_config_migration.py -k "compensate or recover or concurrent or toctou or
    rollback or restore or crash or journal or competitor or one_shot or stale_lock"
      before: 30 passed, 55 deselected
      after : 30 passed, 55 deselected
  Covered compensation/recovery cases include:
    test_compensate_cleanup_backup_tmp_failure
    test_compensate_delete_new_file_failure_keeps_old_copy
    test_compensate_dir_fsync_failure_not_claimed_restored
    test_compensate_when_second_backup_rename_fails
    test_concurrent_migration_lock_blocks_second
    test_crash_after_prepared_journal_recovers_prestate
    test_one_shot_restore_replace_fails_once_then_recovers
    test_one_shot_second_finalize_fails_restores_prestate
    test_recovered_only_on_exact_prestate
    test_recovery_preserves_competitor_mismatched_new_file
    test_isolate_old_source_toctou_preserves_competitor
    test_persistent_recovery_then_next_run_recovers_before_new_migration
  Full migration triad after the change: 111 passed (migration專項 85 passed).
MUTATION: reverting to bare bak.read_bytes() re-raises the finding
  credential_guard/migration.py unresolved_dynamic_sink symbol=_compensate_to_prestate
